Working through each unit against the semantic-linefeeds rule (one sentence per line; rejoin+resplit only at real clause boundaries; list-item commas are not boundaries; don't touch text no finding was raised about).

**Unit 1 & 5** (identical text): 4 sentences are fused. Every candidate (c00–c06) leaves at least one sentence-pair still fused on a single line, so none is a correct complete repair — the full 4-way split isn't offered.

**Unit 2**: only c01 fixes the fused split without introducing a bad break at a list-item comma ("level 1,", "level 2," are enumeration commas, not clause boundaries).

**Unit 3**: c01 splits the fused sentence and rejoins the wrapped remainder (no real boundary inside "need to be able to be ordered... and with a goroutine..." — that `and` joins two prepositional phrases, not two independent clauses).

**Unit 4, 8**: straightforward fused-sentence splits; the split candidate is correct.

**Unit 6**: only the fused sentence needed splitting; line 2 had no finding against it, so the minimal correct fix (c02) leaves it untouched at its existing colon boundary.

**Unit 7**: c01 splits the fused sentence and rejoins "Therefore, we do not usually run..." as one line — splitting between "we" and "do not usually" (c03, original) severs subject from verb, and isolating "Therefore," (c02) severs the adverb from its clause.

